17] [17592186119969 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186318976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119969 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186318977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119969 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186318979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119969 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119969 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119969 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119969 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Steady-State Capacitor Charge in an RCL Circuit: Constant EMF" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119969 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119969 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2448ae49-d644-566d-b4ae-617f4c26be39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119970 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119970 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119970 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119970 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119970 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186318980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119970 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186318981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119970 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186318983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119970 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119970 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119970 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119970 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RCL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119970 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119970 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5db2c6fe-1b26-5daa-b281-d6f6481dc268" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120041 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120041 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120041 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120041 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120041 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120041 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120041 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120041 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120041 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120041 #edb/ref 1045 "Modeling Capacitor Charge in RCL Circuits With Second-Order ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120041 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120041 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120041 #edb/ref 1050 1500.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120041 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4f0b1f3d-0571-55a0-be6f-d9254f9a4842" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120341 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120341 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345014" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120341 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120341 #edb/ref 1071 "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $34\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $\\frac{1}{24}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=12\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120341 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f22963ff-b55b-5a8b-88e3-83527086fce7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120341 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d8303b679d4e00bac0f59f51941a2842473e050033f87f56f099b853431ac5aa.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=34\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=34I=34\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/24)}=24q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=12\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=12\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=12\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=12\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+34\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+24q=9.\n$$\n\nThe *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n12(0)+34(0)+24q=9,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n24q=9\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{9}{24}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{8}$ coulombs." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120953 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120953 #edb/ref 1061 603819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120953 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6520c325-7bbd-5278-b0d0-526cb903b7fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120953 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186120988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120954 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120954 #edb/ref 1061 603820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120954 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "201227a1-db48-5c36-a56f-def337a26a35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120954 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120955 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120955 #edb/ref 1061 603823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120955 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e38418ad-51f1-5041-a5f6-27f9146042f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120955 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186120986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120956 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120956 #edb/ref 1061 603824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120956 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6a229e70-1396-5d2d-b807-48ad2fa1cc31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120956 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186120987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120957 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120957 #edb/ref 1061 603825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120957 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "29e6c1d7-d27e-5ad6-a993-f4c5a1d775a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120957 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120958 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120958 #edb/ref 1061 603828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120958 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "43dcf4f6-4b0d-5b85-899d-d92751a8a1bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120958 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120959 #edb/ref 1061 603832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120959 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "801f1b52-8834-515f-bbfa-c5a084dc6fc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120959 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120961 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120961 #edb/ref 1061 608076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120961 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "27690b14-a860-5a8b-ad9a-d2bcf7d81fcb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120961 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186120990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120986 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have the following RCL circuit.\n\n![](images/d1/d1ce9424aca366d9028319fa2035e01339b26963e39150a8b14b8f2b34d67138.png)\n\nIt is possible to write a differential equation that governs the capacitor charge $q(t)$ in a series RCL circuit.\n\nWe know that the voltage drops across the resistor, capacitor, and inductor, respectively, are\n\n$$\nV_R = RI,\n\\qquad\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C},\n\\qquad\nV_L = L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t},\n$$\n\nwhere $q(t)$ is the charge on the capacitor and $I(t)$ is the circuit current.\n\nAlso, recall that current is the rate at which charge flows, so\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that the source voltage equals the sum of the voltage drops:\n\n$$\nE = V_R + V_C + V_L\n$$\n\nSo, substituting the expressions above gives an equation in $q{:}$\n\n$$\nE = R\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{q}{C} + L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing both sides by $L$ yields the normalized second-order differential equation governing the charge:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + \\dfrac{R}{L}\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{LC}\\,q = \\dfrac{E}{L}\n$$\n\nTo determine a unique solution $q(t),$ we also need initial conditions, typically\n\n$$\nq(0)=q_0,\n\\qquad\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right|_{t=0}=I(0)=I_0.\n$$\n\nLet's now look at a concrete example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120986 #edb/ref 1065 14790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120986 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e689d63b-3192-5e5b-8335-f9ca854a99be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120986 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding an Equation Governing the Capacitor Charge" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120987 #edb/ref 1064 "In the circuit below, we will use Kirchhoff's voltage law to write a differential equation for the capacitor charge $q(t).$\n\n![](images/b9/b9397930e4753a4c5d1945010cc96641b5a6557e0bf521781ff00f3c90fd328f.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs:\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 25I = 25\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{(1/40)} = 40q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 5\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right) = 5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law ($E=V_R + V_C + V_L$), the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n15 = 25\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 40q + 5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $5,$ we obtain the final differential equation for charge:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 8q = 3\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120987 #edb/ref 1065 14792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120987 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bb7ca455-c223-53c8-90df-757518f9e830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120987 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120988 #edb/ref 1064 "An **RCL circuit** is an electrical circuit that contains\n\n- a resistor of resistance $R$, measured in ohms,\n\n- a capacitor of capacitance $C$, measured in farads, and\n\n- an inductor of inductance $L$, measured in henries.\n\nThese circuits are commonly studied in the *series* configuration shown below, with a source electromotive force (EMF) $E$, measured in volts.\n\n![](images/d1/d1ce9424aca366d9028319fa2035e01339b26963e39150a8b14b8f2b34d67138.png)\n\nIn an RCL circuit, the current $I(t),$ measured in amperes, is the rate at which charge flows.\n\nTherefore, if the capacitor charge is $q(t)$ (in coulombs), then the current is found by differentiating:\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nq(t)=2e^{-3t}-5e^{-t}+\\dfrac12,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(2e^{-3t}-5e^{-t}+\\dfrac12\\right)=-6e^{-3t}+5e^{-t}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120988 #edb/ref 1065 14793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120988 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "26e1009b-25c0-5bda-955d-7918f7667683" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120988 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120990 #edb/ref 1064 "For the RCL circuit analyzed previously, the charge $q(t)$ satisfies the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 8q = 3.\n$$\n\nRecall that when we solve the differential equation governing the capacitor charge, the solution is often written in the form\n\n$$\nq(t)=q_c(t)+q_p(t),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $q_c(t)$ is the **transient charge**, which decays to $0$ as $t\\to\\infty,$ and\n\n- $q_p(t)$ is the **steady-state charge**, which governs the long-term behavior of $q(t).$\n\nIn a stable RCL circuit with a *constant* electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, and the steady-state charge is the *constant value* the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} = 0\n$$\n\nTo find the steady-state charge, we substitute these conditions into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0 + 5(0) + 8q = 3\n$$\n\nThis simplifies to\n\n$$\n8q = 3 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad q = \\dfrac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state in which the capacitor is fully charged and the current has ceased to flow. The voltage across the capacitor now exactly balances the source voltage.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is\n$q = \\dfrac{3}{8}\\,\\textrm{C}.$\n\n**Note**: In a stable RCL circuit with a *sinusoidal* electromotive force, the steady-state charge is also sinusoidal." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120990 #edb/ref 1065 14900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120990 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6ab21361-2bac-543c-9a0d-48e35643a23d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120990 #edb/ref 1067 "The Steady-State Capacitor Charge" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316176 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{8}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316176 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316176 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bb542746-c55a-5c6f-8a8c-4f5604d16c42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317000 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186316176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317000 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317000 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317000 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186316176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317000 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c06a3a52-28bd-59ee-a7cc-c175a5c8973c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317928 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317928 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317928 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "69552e60-6c64-5b69-ae78-1dbd1d881d62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318503 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186317928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318503 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318503 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318503 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186317928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318503 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "274bab22-c3a0-54d5-8b68-b9bd43f73177" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318976 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318976 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345122" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318976 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318976 #edb/ref 1071 "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=24.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318976 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7bd95ee-cca5-57b9-9825-7daba3c299ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318976 #edb/ref 1074 "The *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0,\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the steady-state charge, we substitute these values into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=24\n$$\n\nSubstituting the steady-state conditions gives\n\n$$\n0+4(0)+8q=24,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n8q=24\\,⟹\\,q=3.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current $I=\\frac{dq}{dt}$ has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $3$ coulombs." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
